> I was thinking randomly today that the problem with Lojbanquest has
> always been motive; it's *only* about monster-killing, basically.
>
> Some random ideas I've had more-or-less related to that
>
>
> - rooms have various connections that are non-Euclidian (connections
> =A0to words that differ by one letter, connections to the same
> =A0selma'o and (most importantly) connections to the next-most and
> =A0next-least common word)
>
> - monsters get harder in less-commonly-used-word rooms
>
> - random bosses; when you try to go to a room it warns you?; bosses
> =A0are much harder
>
> - Key Idea #1: Rooms only drop the word associated with the room
> =A0name, which forces exploration to be able to make whole sentences
>
> - Key Idea #2: A special/rare treasure is magic *sentences*; if you
> =A0have all the elements of the sentence, the attack is *MUCH* more
> =A0powerful. =A0Using these is essentially required for bosses and the
> =A0higher end rooms, let alone a boss *in* the higher-end rooms. =A0But
> =A0what if your magical sentence needs "zajba"? =A0Well, you'll have to
> =A0get to the zajba room, and defeat something. =A0Major incentive to
> =A0explore.
>
> -Robin
